Several of these run a proposer → challenger → executor family (Clive's Man, Ruth, Lazlo, Doc's Workshop). Invoke the head; it calls its own team. Don't summon a minion directly unless Matthew names it.

### Don't turn this into ceremony

Answer directly — no agent, no subagent — when the job is a single lookup, a one-line factual question, reading a file Matthew already named, or a trivial edit. Route when the work is **substantive and matches a lane**: it needs judgement, touches several files, or changes something worth a paper trail.

If no named agent owns the lane, a generic worker is the right answer. Say so in one line and get on with it.

## When to flag Matthew

Say something briefly, once, if:

- Heavy reasoning or strategy is happening on a build model **and frontier is not in drought** → move it to Sol.
- Drought just engaged or the standing flag flipped **ON** → one line. Challengers
  are on HyperAgent; other heads on Grok. Do not nag to "move it to Sol" until
  Matthew turns drought **OFF**.
- A frontier head is doing mechanical edits → dispatch Grok or Composer instead.
- A task is clearly mechanical and repeatable → offer to hand it to a Composer subagent.
- Website building has started in an expensive frontier chat → suggest Grok.
- Agent or runtime work is going through the coding lane → point back to HyperAgent.

One plain line, not a lecture:

> "Heads up — this is strategy reasoning. Worth doing in Sol rather than here, and I'll keep Grok for the build."

Do not flag on small or ambiguous calls. The goal is protecting speed and spend, not
policing every message.
